              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "PENNYLATT"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I made this stuffing for Thanksgiving and everyone loved it. I mistakenly bought the italian seasoned broth, but used it anyway. Even guests who said they usually don't like stuffing tried it and asked for seconds."
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "booooyaaaa"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Loved it! I doubled poultry seasoning, egg, celery and used Pepperidge Farm cornbread seasoning and fresh mushrooms. I heated stock with butter and folded it into sautéed veggies. Also added 1/2 cup bacon crumbles. I cooked in a Dutch oven at 350 for 25 minutes then another 10 minutes with the lid off to add crunch.",
            "datePublished": "2015-12-31T22:04:54.510Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Pugsleycat"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I made this recipe for a party of roughly 30 people. Everyone loved it! It was also very easy to make. I added carrots, chopped parsley, a bit of ground sage, salt and pepper to taste, and a few tablespoons of milk. Since I have an electric oven I baked it for 45 to 50 minutes instead of the full hour.",
            "datePublished": "2017-11-07T16:30:34.800Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Leslie"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Really good stuffing, but a little bland. I doubled the poultry seasoning and added the same amount of sage.",
            "datePublished": "2018-10-23T23:01:20.513Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Rochelle Cartaciano"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This was my first time making stuffing.. and it was delicious. I used red onion instead, chopped up the celery &amp; carrots really small, added italian seasoning and added Craisins. It wasn't soggy, nor dry. Will be using this again for sure.",
            "datePublished": "2017-01-05T02:35:09.670Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "3"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Zenovia Moroz"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This was a good base to start which is why I gave 3 stars not 5. We like sausage stuffing so I scramble fried sausage, onion and celery plus extra butter with the sausage grease. Then added 2.5 teas of poultry seasoning, some garlic seasoning and ground sea salt &amp; pepper. Finished by adding the low sodium broth and egg. I used bread that was a few days old cut into cubes. There were zero leftovers as everyone loved it including some people who said they don't like stuffing.",
            "datePublished": "2016-10-10T20:41:45.873Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Laurie Jane"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This the basic recipe that I and my mother have always used, except we add about half pound of seasoned pork sausage, sauted with the onion and celery. Nothing better for the bird!",
            "datePublished": "2014-11-27T07:53:29.700Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Luv2Cook4Her"
            },
            "reviewBody": "We love it, so much like the stuffing I grew up with, I boil the giblets with onion and celery to produce the broth that I use in the stuffing, I use 2 cups broth and 2 eggs. I then use some of the giblets in the stuffing and the rest in the gravy. I do spice it up with sage, salt, pepper. Happy Thanksgiving!!",
            "datePublished": "2008-11-25T03:39:00.177Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Shelly"
            },
            "reviewBody": "It is very good. Instead of canned mushrooms I used baby portabella mushrooms that I sauteed with the celery and onion.",
            "datePublished": "2019-11-28T14:39:08.920Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Righteoustoad"
            },
            "reviewBody": "We've made this twice and will continue to adjust it to our taste-buds! Oven-toasted cubes of oatmeal, rye, and pumpernickel bread, used twice the poultry seasoning, turkey broth instead of chicken, and left the mushrooms out of the 2nd batch .We plan on adding cashews and sausage to the next batch. Tastes great out of the oven, but not so great after being refrigerated and microwaved. Still a keeper! Enjoy.",
            "datePublished": "2016-11-29T19:09:36.667Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Sarah Jo"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Tasty. I doubled this, added fresh garlic, increased the butter and omitted the egg. I also added the cut up turkey innards and some chopped chicken/turkey. I kept it in the crockpot to keep it moist and warm until dinner. Mine turned out a little wet, but still good. TIP: Use your own homemade stock. You want it to taste like turkey, not salt.",
            "datePublished": "2008-12-01T11:30:09.647Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Desiri"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I've made this recipe for years with a few tweaks. I use a mix of rye, sour dough, and corn bread, for my bread. And I mix it up the night before to let the flavors meld. Also since I have such a small group- any extra bread I have cut up, I freeze it and have it ready to mix up for Christmas dinner as well. It works out great! It is so yummy and it is why it's been my go to forever now!",
            "datePublished": "2025-11-27T02:26:56.179Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "MirthWater6247"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I have been making this stuffing for years. I have started adding a 1/4c of dried cranberries which has made it even better for our family.",
            "datePublished": "2024-11-28T20:07:46.509Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
